notes.body:
> There is a difference between popup() and exec() that is not obvious at first glance. With exec, the menu exists throughout the execution of attached slots when an action is fired. With popup the Menu is cleared as soon as it is closed. This does not need to be a thing, but it could be. Especially if ->property or ->data is used for QAction or QMenu.

If it is a problem, then I would consider the code in question to be wrong.

> We are already in a different thread than the method which called the menu. Basically you start a sub-thread in a sub-thread with making it a pointer and calling popup.

While the Qt signals and slots are thread safe, there is no need to run things in separate threads. In fact, what you described is not how `QEventLoop` works. The function calling `popup()` (or `exec()`) executes in exactly the same thread. The difference is that `exec()` nests an event loop on top of currently executing one. All actions happen within the same thread.

Consider the Qt 5.12.2 source code:
```
QAction *QMenu::exec(const QPoint &p, QAction *action)
{
    Q_D(QMenu);
    return d->exec(p, action);
}

QAction *QMenuPrivate::exec(const QPoint &p, QAction *action, PositionFunction positionFunction)
{
    Q_Q(QMenu);
    q->ensurePolished();
    q->createWinId();
    QEventLoop evtLoop;
    eventLoop = &evtLoop;
    popup(p, action, positionFunction);

    QPointer<QObject> guard = q;
    (void) evtLoop.exec();
    if (guard.isNull())
        return nullptr;

    action = syncAction;
    syncAction = nullptr;
    eventLoop = nullptr;
    return action;
}
```
Please tell me how `popup()` can do anything more than `exec()`.

